WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.730
- It's about to get even
more country than this cabin,

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:04.150
up in here, alright?

00:00:04.150 --> 00:00:05.980
I've got a friend of
mine, a guy on the line,

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.250
that's going to be joining us.

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.180
He's super talented singer/songwriter,

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:11.690
who's going to be performing
for us a little later as well.

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.543
Thomas Rhett Akins, you there?

00:00:14.655 --> 00:00:15.499
What's up?

00:00:15.499 --> 00:00:16.332
- Good.

00:00:16.332 --> 00:00:17.860
- I like your hat collection, my man.

00:00:17.860 --> 00:00:19.142
- Oh, yeah.

00:00:19.142 --> 00:00:20.213
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:20.213 --> 00:00:21.493
- [Kelly] That's amazing.

00:00:22.433 --> 00:00:25.650
All right, Thomas Rhett, how's
quarantine going at home?

00:00:25.650 --> 00:00:28.420
I've been seeing your
pictures. They're awesome.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:30.460
- Yeah, we're making the
best of it, for sure.

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.130
We're getting really creative, I guess.

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:34.780
With the girls and trying to
figure out new ways to have fun

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.590
and watching a lot of Disney
Plus happening in our house.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:40.470
- Oh my gosh. That's the only two things

00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:42.214
my kids say. Disney Plus.

00:00:42.214 --> 00:00:45.170
Like, I've had to take their iPad away.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.620
I know it's like a way to
give them something to do

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:49.500
so you can actually get stuff done, but--

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:50.333
- Yeah.

00:00:50.333 --> 00:00:52.000
- Man, they are just going nuts right now.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.430
Are your kids going nuts?

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:54.410
- They're going pretty crazy.

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.090
Our kids too, like with the
iPad, like they get on YouTube

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.160
and they go down the worm
hole of the kid videos.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:00.993
- Yep.

00:01:00.993 --> 00:01:01.826
- I start looking at them, and I'm like,

00:01:01.826 --> 00:01:03.410
what are you even watching?

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.260
And I'm like, no more iPad.
This is not gonna work.

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:07.370
- No, I have to take it away.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:09.180
I'm like, I don't know what
my little girl is watching,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.980
but all the sudden she became
like 16 instead of five.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.240
And she has so much attitude.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:16.540
But, it is kind of cool
because we're on the road a lot

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.610
as musicians, so it's kind
of cool to be like, at home

00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:21.770
and kind of forced into this
environment that we don't

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.940
generally get to be a part of.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.459
And you all just had a baby, right?

00:01:26.459 --> 00:01:27.292
- We did.

00:01:27.292 --> 00:01:28.125
- You all got a fresh one out the oven.

00:01:28.125 --> 00:01:28.958
- We (laughs) yeah.

00:01:28.958 --> 00:01:31.230
She's like, she's a little
over two months old.

00:01:31.230 --> 00:01:32.180
Her name is Lennon.

00:01:33.025 --> 00:01:33.858
- So pretty.

00:01:33.858 --> 00:01:36.643
- And last night she slept from like 11:00

00:01:36.643 --> 00:01:38.050
to 7:40 in the morning.

00:01:38.050 --> 00:01:39.160
And it was--

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:39.993
- (Kelly whispers) Yes!

00:01:39.993 --> 00:01:41.640
- It was absolutely mind blowing.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:42.940
It was amazing.

00:01:42.940 --> 00:01:43.970
- Oh my gosh.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:45.320
- Probably one of the best
nights of my life, honestly.

00:01:45.320 --> 00:01:48.690
- That is, I totally feel that.

00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:50.150
It seems like a simple statement,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.862
but that's a glorious victory.

00:01:51.862 --> 00:01:52.695
- It's a big deal.

00:01:52.695 --> 00:01:53.528
- (Kelly laughs)

00:01:53.528 --> 00:01:54.488
- Big deal, for sure.

00:01:54.488 --> 00:01:55.321
- That's a big deal.

00:01:55.321 --> 00:01:57.907
Well, I saw you posted a photo
of how you handle spit up.

00:01:57.907 --> 00:02:00.950
(Dog barks) Oh, sorry, my dog
just barked at my husband.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.260
So apparently he's evil.
- It's okay, our dog--

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:06.490
- No, sorry, he was barking.

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:11.490
But I saw this photo that
you posted and have you just

00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:12.470
given up on shirts?

00:02:12.470 --> 00:02:15.513
Because like you've got vomit
going down the back of you.

00:02:15.513 --> 00:02:17.810
- No, you know what, it's
funny is someone asked me that

00:02:17.810 --> 00:02:18.643
the other day.

00:02:18.643 --> 00:02:20.686
They go, "Do you ever wear a shirt?"

00:02:20.686 --> 00:02:23.770
And when I'm at home, the
answer is not much of the time.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:24.940
Because I guess when I
work out of the house

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.390
and I work out without a shirt on and

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:28.323
then I don't shower until, you
know, seven o'clock at night.

00:02:28.323 --> 00:02:30.259
Just kind of leave the shirt off.

00:02:30.259 --> 00:02:31.092
- Yeah.

00:02:31.092 --> 00:02:32.270
- Especially when I'm holding
the baby, I mean there's no

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:34.320
point in ruining, you know,
eight shirts during the day.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.440
I think my wife changes about 17 times

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:37.546
just from spit up, so.

00:02:37.546 --> 00:02:40.220
- Oh my gosh. I gave up on nice things.

00:02:40.220 --> 00:02:41.690
I just own black or white.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:43.030
I'm just like, whatever.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:46.070
So I saw a video showing you outdoors,

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:48.150
while you are quarantining.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.290
What were you doing in a tree?

00:02:50.290 --> 00:02:51.570
Like I saw this video of you

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.623
like trying to swing like Tarzan.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:55.737
What was the deal with that?

00:02:55.737 --> 00:02:57.810
- We just moved into a new house

00:02:57.810 --> 00:02:59.480
and we were kind of exploring outside

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:02.500
and my wife noticed this
vine hanging from the tree

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.460
and my kids were trying to swing on it

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:06.540
and Lauren was like, I used
to do that when I was little.

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:07.876
She was like let me go try it

00:03:07.876 --> 00:03:09.290
and I said "how about you
let me go try it first

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:11.420
just to make sure that it's like stable."

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:13.150
And so I like yanked
on it a couple of times

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:14.930
and it felt fine and
then I like went for it,

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:17.530
and the limb just snapped
in half and I think

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.840
I bruised my tailbone and
I'm really embarrassed

00:03:20.840 --> 00:03:22.740
that Lauren caught that
on video, but you know,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.730
that's just what we do around here.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:27.250
- Is your wife there? Is
that who you keep looking at?

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:28.692
- Yeah, she is. You
want to come over here?

00:03:28.692 --> 00:03:30.530
- Hey, what's up?

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.330
- She was doing the girls' hair.

00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:33.651
- Oh, making it pretty?

00:03:33.651 --> 00:03:34.484
- Hey.

00:03:34.484 --> 00:03:36.510
- Hi. Do your girls ask for braids?

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:38.530
My girl asks for braids all the time.

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.690
- Yes, Elsa braids. Elsa and Anna braids,

00:03:40.690 --> 00:03:41.777
is what they call them.
- Exactly.

00:03:41.777 --> 00:03:43.450
And I keep telling my
girl, like you don't have

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:44.530
that hair yet. Okay?

00:03:44.530 --> 00:03:46.360
Like I can do a braid,
but you ain't got that.

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:47.540
That's not happening yet.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:49.730
So, that's amazing.

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.000
Hey, well how are you doing
as a mamma? This is a lot.

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.620
- Yeah. Honestly, we're good.

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:59.785
We're going on like 30,
or no, 40 something days

00:03:59.785 --> 00:04:00.618
- 40 days.

00:04:00.618 --> 00:04:02.870
Quarantine because our kids
had the flu the week before

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:05.057
coronavirus hit, so

00:04:05.057 --> 00:04:05.890
- Yeah.

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:07.710
- But my sister and her
husband live close by,

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:10.790
and my best friend lives close
by, and they all were able

00:04:10.790 --> 00:04:13.160
to work from home like super
early on, so they were like

00:04:13.160 --> 00:04:14.860
we're going to come
quarantine at your house.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:15.890
So they have been here
- Oh

00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:19.970
- I think they said
yesterday it was 34 days.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:20.980
That they had been here.

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:22.460
- We've had a lot of
help, which has been nice.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:23.437
And a lot of (mumble).

00:04:23.437 --> 00:04:24.870
- I was going to say, that's
incredibly cool because

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.740
I was going to say you all
being like, I know you have

00:04:27.740 --> 00:04:30.210
already been parents but a baby is a lot,

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:33.098
and especially in this time.
- It's tough.

00:04:33.098 --> 00:04:35.020
That's a lot just to get, and
I know that sounds horrible,

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:36.360
but every parent's thinking it.

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.870
Sometimes you just need to be alone.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:39.703
Without them.

00:04:39.703 --> 00:04:41.660
- Just to like come back and be fresh

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:43.897
and not hate your life.

00:04:43.897 --> 00:04:44.980
- Absolutely.

00:04:47.105 --> 00:04:50.030
- We've had lots of extra
sets of hands around.

00:04:50.030 --> 00:04:52.730
To hold the baby, while
somebody cooks or entertain

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.540
the toddlers, or you know.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.745
- That's so cool.

00:04:56.745 --> 00:04:57.620
- It's been fun. Lots of games.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:04:58.453
- Well, that's kind of cool.

00:04:58.453 --> 00:05:00.235
You've got your own quarantine community.

00:05:00.235 --> 00:05:01.265
I like that.

00:05:01.265 --> 00:05:02.098
- Yeah.

00:05:03.087 --> 00:05:05.250
- So, you studied to be
a nurse, right, Lauren?

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.010
And I heard you celebrated
Thomas' 30th birthday

00:05:08.010 --> 00:05:10.060
by surprising some nursing students.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:11.630
That was cool.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.130
- We did. It was sweet.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:14.440
It was fun.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:16.180
- How did that come about?

00:05:16.180 --> 00:05:18.650
- I'd stayed connected
with the nursing school.

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:22.720
I went to the University
of Tennessee in Knoxville.

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:25.400
And I have just stayed really
close with that community

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:27.400
and they were like would you
all mind calling in and just

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:29.750
encouraging some of the
students and, I mean,

00:05:29.750 --> 00:05:33.020
I can't imagine, my brother
is actually a senior this year

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:38.020
at UT and I just can't imagine
that being in college and

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.480
especially in high school,
there's so many like last things

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:44.780
that so many kids
- Yeah, that are kind of

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:46.900
getting ripped away from people. Yeah.

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:49.790
- But I do think people are
doing a really good job at just

00:05:49.790 --> 00:05:52.213
finding creative ways to celebrate.

00:05:53.223 --> 00:05:56.530
It will definitely be for
all these students such an

00:05:56.530 --> 00:05:58.695
unforgettable senior year.

00:05:58.695 --> 00:05:59.820
- Yeah, for sure.

00:05:59.820 --> 00:06:03.220
- It was cool to get to
at least try to encourage.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:04.053
We have no idea

00:06:04.053 --> 00:06:05.830
what they are feeling, but I'm sure,

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:07.080
there's so many unknowns.

00:06:09.208 --> 00:06:11.260
- It was nice because it
was a thoughtful thing.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:14.320
And I think that the, for
everybody like that putting their

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:16.320
life on the line right now for us.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:18.277
That's a lot to ask of a human.

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:19.110
- It is.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:20.170
- And a heart.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:22.370
I love your love story.

00:06:22.370 --> 00:06:26.640
Would you tell it for us?
Tell everyone how you met.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:29.203
And where you were like,
he's the one, Lauren.

00:06:30.925 --> 00:06:32.225
- It was one summer night.

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:37.824
- No but you all were really young.

00:06:37.824 --> 00:06:40.380
- We have known each other
for a really long time.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:42.238
- Like how long? Tell
everyone how long you all have

00:06:42.238 --> 00:06:43.216
known each other.

00:06:43.216 --> 00:06:44.945
- We've known each other-
- First grade.

00:06:44.945 --> 00:06:46.040
- First grade
- Technically

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:47.020
- We started going to school together.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:48.440
I don't really know how
much you can know somebody

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:49.273
in first grade.

00:06:49.273 --> 00:06:51.540
- Yeah, it was middle school
when we like really got

00:06:51.540 --> 00:06:53.950
to be close, hung out a whole lot more,

00:06:53.950 --> 00:06:58.100
and church camp was kind of
the start of the friendship

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.530
in middle school, but then it just--

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:01.750
- Watch out for church camp.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:03.047
That's where people be making out.

00:07:03.047 --> 00:07:04.297
- That's right.

00:07:05.527 --> 00:07:08.985
- Finding the Lord and a girlfriend.

00:07:08.985 --> 00:07:11.530
- Yeah, there's a picture of
us somewhere, of me and Lauren

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:13.420
at church camp. I think we
were fifth grade, sixth grade.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:15.986
But I was like a foot
shorter than Lauren was.

00:07:15.986 --> 00:07:16.819
- Yeah.

00:07:16.819 --> 00:07:20.610
- And I dyed my hair black
because I was in this like punk

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.430
rock band and, you know, I
just was obsessed with her

00:07:24.430 --> 00:07:27.230
and I'm sure she looked at
me like "yeah, probably not."

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:31.670
But we became really good
friends that year and actually

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:33.420
dated a little bit when we were 16.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:36.413
- It wasn't good.

00:07:36.413 --> 00:07:40.150
- I was a needy boyfriend.
If that makes any sense.

00:07:40.150 --> 00:07:41.830
- Well, you're also 16.

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.540
So you're still like in puberty too.

00:07:44.540 --> 00:07:45.890
- Exactly. Figuring it out.

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:48.930
- Did everybody tell you it was not

00:07:48.930 --> 00:07:50.248
gonna work out because that's

00:07:50.248 --> 00:07:51.120
- Absolutely.

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:53.940
- That's weird that it did. It's awesome.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.190
- Yeah, so we broke up but still remained

00:07:56.190 --> 00:07:58.890
really good friends and
both dated other people from

00:07:58.890 --> 00:08:00.816
us five or six years.

00:08:00.816 --> 00:08:03.950
Through all of that time, we
went on double dates together

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:05.547
with other people that we were dating.

00:08:05.547 --> 00:08:07.720
- He would come stay with me
and my roommates in college.

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:10.250
Because he went to a school
in Nashville and we were

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:12.600
in Knoxville which is
roughly a three hour drive.

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:14.700
So on the weekends, he would
come up and go to games

00:08:14.700 --> 00:08:16.190
and stay with us.

00:08:16.190 --> 00:08:19.560
And I think a couple of times
he stayed with my boyfriend,

00:08:19.560 --> 00:08:20.624
we just-

00:08:20.624 --> 00:08:22.480
- (Laughing) I love this.

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:26.600
- Yeah. We are a very tight
knit circle we run with so.

00:08:26.600 --> 00:08:28.050
- Lauren, I also wanted to
bring this up because you

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:30.980
just wrote a book called
"Live in Love" about your life

00:08:30.980 --> 00:08:32.483
and family.

00:08:32.483 --> 00:08:33.890
So what made you want to write it?

00:08:33.890 --> 00:08:36.290
Because that's a lot to
let people in your life.

00:08:36.290 --> 00:08:37.603
- Yep. It is.

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:43.830
You know, I think marrying
him was a pretty big leap

00:08:44.470 --> 00:08:47.330
of faith as far as knowing
which direction he was trying

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:48.433
to go with his career.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:55.730
His whole world is super out
of my box and so writing a book

00:08:56.090 --> 00:09:00.770
was not something that I ever
had like on my dream checklist

00:09:00.770 --> 00:09:04.180
but I did I wanted to share my story.

00:09:04.180 --> 00:09:06.670
I was encouraged to share my story.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:10.740
I didn't think I would
ever would in this capacity

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:15.740
but I actually went in to my
agency's office just to talk

00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:19.620
through possibly writing a
children's book because we were

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:22.123
in the process of adopting Willa Gray.

00:09:23.422 --> 00:09:24.363
And they were like it
would be cool if you wrote

00:09:24.363 --> 00:09:26.720
a children's book that
had to do with Africa,

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:27.890
African themed,

00:09:27.890 --> 00:09:29.980
so we kind of went in talking
about that and she was like

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:32.090
well tell me a little about yourself.

00:09:32.090 --> 00:09:36.020
As I was like talking through
how I was raised and some

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:38.420
of the stories throughout my
life and kind of what had led

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:42.470
me to where I was now, I'm
pretty sure she had cried

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:44.940
like three times throughout
me telling my story.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:46.200
I was like is this normal?

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:48.090
- It moved her.

00:09:48.090 --> 00:09:50.247
- And she was like wiping her eyes

00:09:50.247 --> 00:09:51.120
and she was like that's it.

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:53.150
Like you have to tell that story first.

00:09:53.150 --> 00:09:55.020
We'll get to children's books later.

00:09:55.020 --> 00:09:57.293
But I was like, really?

00:09:58.450 --> 00:10:01.160
And our manager, who is one
of our very best friends,

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:03.200
she was like, Lauren, I've
been telling you for awhile

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:04.740
you need to be writing this stuff down.

00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:07.763
There's so many good stories in your life.

00:10:09.098 --> 00:10:11.440
So that's really what started it.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:13.890
It turned into a full-on book.

00:10:13.890 --> 00:10:16.160
- It's about your life and
kind of how you've handled it

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:17.750
and navigated it, right?

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:19.154
- Right.
- Living in love.

00:10:19.154 --> 00:10:24.110
- Marriage and our kids,
adoption, biological.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:25.130
How I was raised.

00:10:25.130 --> 00:10:28.180
Kind of what our life is like now.

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:29.980
It was a really therapeutic process,

00:10:29.980 --> 00:10:30.880
going back through

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:32.690
all of that.
- For both of us. Sure.

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:33.523
- I'll bet.

00:10:33.523 --> 00:10:35.811
- But it was good. It's scary.

00:10:35.811 --> 00:10:39.100
I would be lying if I
told you I wasn't anxious

00:10:39.100 --> 00:10:41.770
and nervous about this coming
out, just because it is

00:10:41.770 --> 00:10:42.603
really personal.

00:10:42.603 --> 00:10:43.860
It's really honest.

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:48.750
And this is so not my comfort
zone, just being in this world

00:10:48.750 --> 00:10:50.620
and being super vulnerable to people

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:52.700
that you may never meet.

00:10:52.700 --> 00:10:56.720
But it's been a really cool,
I've been surprised at how cool

00:10:56.720 --> 00:11:00.470
the process has been, and how
excited I do feel, although

00:11:00.470 --> 00:11:02.760
the nerves are still definitely there.

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.230
- Connecting to people through
your book is such a powerful

00:11:05.230 --> 00:11:07.520
thing because a lot of
people go through things

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:11.170
with blended families and
everybody's from a different kind

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:13.555
of place, and that can be very hard.

00:11:13.555 --> 00:11:14.820
- For sure.

00:11:14.820 --> 00:11:16.400
- Why did you adopt first?

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:17.780
You adopted first, right?

00:11:17.780 --> 00:11:21.100
- We did. I had always talked
about adopting my whole life.

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:22.843
My mom is actually adopted.

00:11:23.960 --> 00:11:26.850
I just thought it was really
cool, so we talked about it

00:11:26.850 --> 00:11:30.270
off and on but it wasn't
something that we had sat down

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.773
and had a like a full-on
adoption conversation.

00:11:34.625 --> 00:11:37.757
And then I was in Uganda in 2016

00:11:39.325 --> 00:11:40.158
- Yeah.

00:11:40.158 --> 00:11:44.070
- And I had a picture of
one of the babies there.

00:11:44.070 --> 00:11:47.970
And I had shared it on social media

00:11:47.970 --> 00:11:50.420
and when I finally got to
FaceTime him that night

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.470
I was telling him her story
and I was like, "Babe, we know

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:58.030
so many people who are
trying to adopt right now

00:11:58.030 --> 00:12:01.077
and this little girl
needs a forever home."

00:12:02.630 --> 00:12:06.150
I was like so moved I mean
the second I touched her

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:09.030
it was like electric and
I was like "oh, my word.

00:12:09.030 --> 00:12:11.860
This little girl has just taken my heart."

00:12:11.860 --> 00:12:14.840
And I was like, "Honey, we have
got to find her her forever

00:12:14.840 --> 00:12:17.850
home," like I know that's why
I'm here is to get this girl

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:18.893
to her home.

00:12:20.984 --> 00:12:21.934
- Oh, my God. Okay.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:25.220
You totally Oprah-ed me just now.

00:12:25.220 --> 00:12:26.563
I'm like, okay.

00:12:27.493 --> 00:12:30.263
I did my own make up and
this is not waterproof.

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:33.370
Like it's so beautiful
because I know that feeling

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:35.980
because the moment you
touch them or hold somebody

00:12:35.980 --> 00:12:37.960
it doesn't matter if it's yours or not.

00:12:37.960 --> 00:12:39.110
We're a blended family.

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.300
This is my purpose.

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:43.790
It's such a powerful thing.

00:12:43.790 --> 00:12:44.964
- For sure. For sure.

00:12:44.964 --> 00:12:47.114
- So how did that turn
into ya'll adopting?

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:50.360
- When I talked to her
on the phone that night

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:52.800
and she said we needed to
find her a home, I literally,

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:55.380
it was after a concert,
and I was going to bed,

00:12:55.380 --> 00:12:57.842
and I was like, "we'll bring her home."

00:12:57.842 --> 00:12:59.850
And I don't fully remember even saying it.

00:12:59.850 --> 00:13:03.440
It was like such a spiritual
thing for me that it just

00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:04.950
came out of my body.

00:13:04.950 --> 00:13:08.840
And then literally two weeks
after, we were having home

00:13:08.840 --> 00:13:12.930
assessments and talking to
adoption agencies and then

00:13:12.930 --> 00:13:15.140
I got to go to Uganda maybe

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:15.973
- October

00:13:15.973 --> 00:13:18.060
- Yeah, like six months later.

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:19.800
And then I got to meet her
for the first time and that's

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:22.290
when we really started
the process and we were--

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:26.320
Lauren was there for like a
total of a very long time.

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:29.040
And I was going to Uganda, I
remember I played a show in

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:31.870
Phoenix, Arizona and flew
from Phoenix to Uganda,

00:13:31.870 --> 00:13:34.610
stayed for a week, came
back home, played Nashville,

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:36.080
and then came right back to Uganda.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.850
And it was like this
really, really strange time

00:13:38.850 --> 00:13:43.510
of like I'm still doing
touring things and Lauren is

00:13:43.510 --> 00:13:45.690
in the middle of all of this
and then fast-forward like

00:13:45.690 --> 00:13:48.453
three months later we're
pregnant in Uganda.

00:13:49.372 --> 00:13:52.460
- (laughing)
- Ugandan toilets

00:13:54.035 --> 00:13:56.030
- I remember playing the
Nashville show and I was talking

00:13:56.030 --> 00:13:57.200
to her and I was like
"what are you doing?"

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.980
And she was like "I'm currently
just yacking in the toilet."

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:00.230
She's like, "what are you doing?"

00:14:00.230 --> 00:14:01.680
Well, I'm hanging out with
all of your best friends

00:14:01.680 --> 00:14:02.513
in Nashville.

00:14:04.390 --> 00:14:05.300
You'll see this in the book.

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:06.680
Like I look like a total jerk.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:07.513
- No.

00:14:07.513 --> 00:14:09.110
- Seventeen million times in the book.

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.650
And I probably am a lot of the time.

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.038
- You're also doing your job,
like that's how you provide.

00:14:14.038 --> 00:14:14.871
- Yeah.

00:14:14.871 --> 00:14:15.704
- Provide for your family.

00:14:15.704 --> 00:14:17.511
And then I just got to say
before I move on to something

00:14:17.511 --> 00:14:18.480
else I love about you all.

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:20.950
It's a really beautiful thing
that I think I believe that

00:14:20.950 --> 00:14:22.860
things in life are supposed
to happen when they happen.

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:24.490
Like regardless if it's good or not.

00:14:24.490 --> 00:14:26.580
It's just how it is and you handle it.

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:29.090
And I love how you said
"It was just something

00:14:29.090 --> 00:14:29.923
that came out of you."

00:14:29.923 --> 00:14:32.540
Because I think that says a
lot about both of your hearts,

00:14:32.540 --> 00:14:35.137
it's that you noticed there
was a need for something

00:14:35.137 --> 00:14:36.393
and you fulfilled it.

00:14:36.393 --> 00:14:37.226
- Yeah.

00:14:37.226 --> 00:14:38.993
- And it wasn't a question.

00:14:38.993 --> 00:14:41.160
you know, it was like
this just needs to happen.

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:43.490
I think that's a really
beautiful thing and how we should

00:14:43.490 --> 00:14:44.920
live our lives.

00:14:44.920 --> 00:14:47.190
All right, so Thomas the song
that you're going to do for us

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:48.940
later is called "Be a Light."

00:14:48.940 --> 00:14:50.660
It's a great anthem for
the world right now,

00:14:50.660 --> 00:14:52.020
like how did this song come about?

00:14:52.020 --> 00:14:53.950
I know there's a lot of
artists on it, right?

00:14:53.950 --> 00:14:56.690
- Yeah. I actually wrote
this song last year

00:14:56.690 --> 00:14:58.660
in a whole different mind
set, like having no clue

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:01.720
that COVID-19 was about to hit the world.

00:15:01.720 --> 00:15:02.600
- Yeah.

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.700
- So it was going to kind
of be on like our next album

00:15:04.700 --> 00:15:06.440
that we're going to start
working on at some point

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:08.770
in the near future, hopefully,
but just when all of this

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:10.887
started happening I called
my label and I was like,

00:15:10.887 --> 00:15:12.660
"Hey, I think this song
needs to come out now.

00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:14.870
Like I think the world
needs to hear this song."

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:16.670
Because it's a really
encouraging song just about being

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:18.710
a light in a dark place,
and I kind of think that's

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:21.040
kind of the world we're in right now.

00:15:21.040 --> 00:15:23.450
So we decided to put it
out and we got Reba on it,

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:26.300
Keith Urban, Chris
Thomlin, and Hillary Scott

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:29.383
are singing on it with me,
which was super special

00:15:29.383 --> 00:15:31.830
and a bunch of the proceeds
are going to the Music Cares

00:15:31.830 --> 00:15:33.720
COVID Relief Fund, which is really cool.

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:37.640
So whether you stream it on
any platform or YouTube it

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:41.797
or download it that money
will go to help a lot

00:15:41.797 --> 00:15:43.300
of the people.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:45.430
- All right. Well thank you guys so much.

00:15:45.430 --> 00:15:47.610
We are going to get to hear
Thomas Rhett sing later

00:15:47.610 --> 00:15:49.357
in the show, very excited about that.

00:15:49.357 --> 00:15:51.240
The song is called, "Be a Light."

00:15:51.240 --> 00:15:52.510
So please go download it now

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.650
and also go order Lauren's book.

00:15:54.650 --> 00:15:55.750
You can pre-order it right now.

00:15:55.750 --> 00:15:59.180
It's called "Live in Love"
or you can get it August 18.

00:15:59.180 --> 00:16:00.830
It's a great, great message.

00:16:00.830 --> 00:16:03.580
You're going to love it.
You're going to connect to it.

